# Labs 512-label spike diagnostic interpretation (2026-10-22)

**Result.** The bounded trace completed, but the original 113 ms regime did not recur. Its stock arm has retained p50/p90/p95 of **9.463 / 10.635 / 10.924 ms**; the 20-extra-untimed-call arm has **8.492 / 8.878 / 9.055 ms**. No call or raw Labs sample in either arm exceeded 50 ms. This supports a narrow finding that the normal one-call warmup leaves ordinary 512-label timings sensitive to additional warmup in this instrumented sitting. It does **not** identify the cause of the saved candidate's high block, justify rejecting that block, or replace the frozen admission matrix with these favorable values.

## Verified trace identity and observations

The completed receipt is `labs-spike-diagnostic-final/receipt.json`, SHA-256 `826810ed45df1a386d40c1b28c2b6ffefcb40b033ebde5d1536240d3a0496a55`. It records the exact frozen candidate archive SHA-256 `06fe22b7f05dceb27f59b2e82d33fab541c8cee04c37b1a1ccdf078f6ecdc9be`, the authenticated installed `dist`, the pinned Labs 0.9.0 sources, and a fixed saved 20-raw-sample single-call plan. Eight pairs alternate child order; each block is a fresh process. This intentionally omits a new pilot and the original first-block 0.5-second stopping rule, as the receipt and generated README disclose.

I read all 16 child files and checked each file hash against the receipt, target alias, arm/pair identity, saved plan, 20 chronological raw samples, 16 exact trim survivors, and an empty trace-fault list. All checks passed. The call ledger has 21 entries per stock child and 41 per warmed child, totaling 168 and 328 calls. Every raw sample's callback index points to the matching call, and its callback interval falls inside Labs' recorded `t0/t1` interval. Every child has 80 forced-GC edge markers, corresponding to before/after start/end marks for 20 samples.

| Diagnostic arm | Retained p50 / p90 / p95 | Highest raw Labs sample | Highest callback wall interval | Raw samples or calls above 50 ms |
| --- | --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| Stock | 9.463 / 10.635 / 10.924 ms | 16.017 ms | 19.431 ms | 0 |
| 20 extra untimed calls | 8.492 / 8.878 / 9.055 ms | 11.822 ms | 24.470 ms | 0 |

All eight paired stock block medians exceed their warmed counterpart by **0.655–1.388 ms**. This is a repeatable direction within this sitting, across alternating arm order, and shows a warmup effect on the measured ordinary regime. It does not show that extra warmup prevents the saved high regime, because there was no concurrent stock recurrence. The saved candidate's exact retained p50/p90/p95 remains **10.295 / 77.719 / 117.499 ms**, with one block median at **113.293 ms**.

With all clocks converted to milliseconds in each child, none of the 320 timed callback windows or wider Labs `t0/t1` sample windows overlaps an **observed** PerformanceObserver GC entry. Untimed warmup calls do overlap observed events (3 of 8 stock first calls and 36 of 168 warmed calls). These are limited observations: GC delivery can be incomplete even after the bounded `takeRecords()` drain, and the trace records numerous GC events plus forced collections around samples. Process CPU exceeds callback wall time for all 320 timed calls because the counter includes background threads; it cannot be treated as callback-core CPU or used to rule out scheduling. Context-switch deltas likewise cover the process and cannot alone identify a suspended callback. The added trace records no memory or optimization events. Preserved original Labs trial stats do include `heap` min/max/p50 fields; those V8 heap summaries are outside the exact-owned-system memory comparison and are not used as memory admission evidence. The largest samples here track callback wall closely, without a large Labs-only timing gap, but they are far below the original unexplained tail.

## Runner action supported by this result

A focused runner change is warranted to remove the known warmup-policy weakness: add an explicit fixed **untimed** warmup count for `single-call` trials in the locally patched Labs `benchFn` path, after the original first-result capture and before the timed loop, independent of the current 500,000 ns threshold. Preserve each benchmark's return/consume/after behavior, callback state parity, fresh-child scheduling, sample count, GC policy, timer, and trim. Apply one chosen count uniformly to candidate, main, and published arms, verify stateful fixtures, then run a new matched matrix and retain its provenance separately from the frozen one. The present 20-call arm is evidence for testing that policy; it is not yet a calibrated universal warmup count or an admission result.

If a high stock regime recurs in a later targeted sitting, use the aligned raw Labs and callback intervals to separate measurement overhead from callback wall time, then obtain a dedicated scheduling/CPU or V8 trace before attributing its mechanism. The original 113 ms episode remains unresolved after this no-recurrence diagnostic.
